{"name":"Diffused Light","hex":"#ebe5d5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"diffused","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of diffuse"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having spread widely."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/diffused"},{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"}],"entries":[{"title":"Molecular diffusion","blocks":[{"text":"Molecular diffusion is the motion of atoms, molecules, or other particles of a gas or liquid at temperatures above absolute zero. The rate of this movement is a function of temperature, viscosity of the fluid, size and density of the particles. This type of diffusion explains the net flux of molecules from a region of higher concentration to one of lower concentration."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/DiffusionMicroMacro.gif/330px-DiffusionMicroMacro.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molecular_diffusion"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.